1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,759
Synchronous APIs couple latency: your
checkout waits on inventory. Sometimes you
need facts to propagate without blocking
the caller.

2
00:00:07,879 --> 00:00:21,440
Events are facts — something that happened
— and consumers must tolerate at-least-
once delivery. Assuming exactly-once is
how systems lie to themselves. Distinguish
facts from commands. OrderCreated is a
fact.

3
00:00:21,559 --> 00:00:35,399
CreateOrder is a command. Facts let
multiple consumers react — email,
analytics, inventory — without the
producer knowing each one. Emitting
commands disguised as events couples
producers to consumer intent.

4
00:00:35,519 --> 00:00:46,359
Emit what happened; let consumers decide
what to do. Dual writes without an outbox
are a classic failure. Transaction commits
the order. Process crashes before Kafka
send returns.

5
00:00:46,479 --> 00:00:59,799
Customer sees success; inventory never
hears. The outbox pattern records the
state change and the event in one
transaction; a publisher relays outbox
rows to the bus. Look at this java for a
moment. Why outbox?

6
00:00:59,979 --> 00:01:10,060
Atomically record state change plus event
to avoid lost messages. Dual write looks
shorter in a demo; under failure
injection, outbox wins.

7
00:01:10,179 --> 00:01:22,619
Outbox publishers need their own
monitoring: lag, failure counts, poison
messages. An outbox that stops draining is
a silent outage — the API looks healthy
while downstream consumers starve.

8
00:01:22,739 --> 00:01:29,060
At-least-once delivery means duplicates
are normal. Consumers must treat
redelivery as expected.

9
00:01:29,179 --> 00:01:46,419
Idempotency keys, dedupe tables, or
natural unique constraints — upserting
state keyed by order id — turn duplicates
into no-ops. Test redelivery in CI.
Untested idempotency is hopeful naming.

10
00:01:46,539 --> 00:01:58,899
Exactly-once end-to-end across independent
systems is a marketing phrase more often
than an engineering guarantee. Ordering is
partial: across partitions or consumers,
time is not a single global line.

11
00:01:59,019 --> 00:02:05,619
Design workflows that tolerate inventory-
before-email unless you intentionally
serialize through a single key and
partition.

12
00:02:05,739 --> 00:02:15,579
If your business needs strong global order
or a saga with orchestration, say so — do
not pretend a firehose of events gives you
global transaction semantics for free.

13
00:02:15,699 --> 00:02:22,419
Schema evolution needs compatibility
rules: additive fields, careful renames,
consumers that ignore unknowns.

14
00:02:22,539 --> 00:02:31,299
Prefer expand/contract: add fields, deploy
consumers that tolerate them, then switch
producers, then remove old fields later.

15
00:02:31,419 --> 00:02:39,899
Fat events that embed every nested entity
make evolution and PII handling harder —
every consumer breaks when pricing shape
changes.

16
00:02:40,019 --> 00:02:49,979
Prefer identifiers plus essential
attributes; let consumers query details or
maintain their own read models from
smaller events. Connect events to
observability.

17
00:02:50,099 --> 00:02:57,259
Every event should carry correlation ids
so a checkout trace includes the async
legs that fire after the HTTP response.

18
00:02:57,379 --> 00:03:08,460
When events cross team boundaries,
ownership of schema becomes political —
decide who may add fields and how
compatibility is tested. Technology alone
does not solve governance.

19
00:03:08,579 --> 00:03:19,139
Events are facts; consumers must tolerate
at-least-once; outbox protects producers
from dual-write loss. That triad is the
episode in one breath.

20
00:03:19,259 --> 00:03:28,179
Event-driven designs shine when they
reduce coupling and absorb load spikes.
They punish teams that skip idempotency,
outbox, and observability.

21
00:03:28,299 --> 00:03:37,179
Performance across sync and async paths
still needs a disciplined loop — measure,
hypothesize, change one thing, remeasure.
